The Responses API returns web citations as **annotations on output text**, not as a separate top-level citations array. Enable search with `tools: [{ type: "web_search" }]` ([official quickstart](https://platform.openai.com/docs/quickstart/make-your-first-api-request)).

- `response.output` contains output items, including the assistant `message` and possibly a `web_search_call`.
- A message’s `content` contains an `output_text` part.
- `content.annotations` contains citation objects with `type: "url_citation"`, `url`, `title`, `start_index`, and `end_index`. The indexes identify the cited span in the text ([API reference](https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference/responses-streaming/response/output_text/annotation/added?lang=node.js)).
- `response.output_text` is a convenient plain-text aggregate; inspect `output` to obtain citation metadata.

```js
import OpenAI from "openai";

const client = new OpenAI();

const response = await client.responses.create({
  model: "gpt-5",
  tools: [{ type: "web_search" }],
  input: "What was a positive news story from today?",
});

console.log(response.output_text);

for (const item of response.output ?? []) {
  for (const part of item.content ?? []) {
    for (const citation of part.annotations ?? []) {
      if (citation.type === "url_citation") {
        console.log(citation.title, citation.url);
      }
    }
  }
}
```

